Soluble compounds dissolve in water, whereas insoluble compounds do not. For example, barium chromate (\ce{BaCrO4_{(s)}}) is an insoluble salt, whereas \ce{NaCl} is a soluble salt. The soluble character of a compound in water can be prediced with the help of Table \ref{tab:Table-Solubility}. To use this table, you need to start by assessing the right ion (the anion, \ce{CrO4^{2-}}) located in the left column of the Table, to then assess the left ion (the cation, \ce{Ba^{2+}}) located in the right column. Using this Table you can hence predict that chromate is an insoluble compound.
Let us work on another example, and predict for example the soluble character of \ce{CaSO4}, calcium sulfate. We start by looking for \ce{SO4^{2-}} in the left column to find out is soluble. Next, we continue in the same line as \ce{SO4^{2-}} and look for the ion in the left \ce{Ca^{2+}}. In conclusion, even when \ce{SO4^{2-}}  is soluble, when combined with \ce{Ca^{2+}}, we have that \ce{CaSO4} is insoluble, and overall \ce{CaSO4_{(s)}} is insoluble.
